Feature: Staging on AWS, defined in CDK and deployed from GitHub (docs/production.md, phases 5 and 6)
  The app runs on AWS in Ireland (eu-west-1) at a public HTTPS address, so it can be tried as it will run in
  production: real sign-up and login, PostgreSQL, the production profile. Everything in AWS is described in code
  (infra/, AWS CDK in TypeScript) and changed only by deploying that code, which GitHub does after every green CI run on
  main. This is staging: one environment, sized small to keep the bill low; production comes later as a second
  environment from the same code.

  # --- What runs where ---

  Scenario: The site answers at a public HTTPS address
    When I open the staging URL (a CloudFront address, https://<id>.cloudfront.net, until a domain is bought)
    Then I see the landing page over HTTPS
    And plain HTTP is redirected to HTTPS

  Scenario: Accounts are real: sign up, log out, log in
    Given the staging site
    When I sign up with an email and password
    Then I am signed in and land in onboarding
    And after logging out I can log in again with the same email and password
    And a wrong password is refused
    And nobody is signed in automatically: the test-account auto-login is off (the prod profile, and AUTO_LOGIN=false)

  Scenario: Accounts and content live in AWS, in Ireland
    Then the database is PostgreSQL on Amazon RDS in eu-west-1, in subnets with no route to or from the internet
    And only the backend can reach it
    And its password and the login-token secret are generated by AWS Secrets Manager and never appear in the code,
      the repository or the deploy logs
    And the database is kept when the app is redeployed, and snapshotted if the stack is ever deleted

  Scenario: The browser only ever talks to the frontend
    Then CloudFront forwards everything to a load balancer in front of the frontend
    And the backend has no public address: the frontend reaches it on a private name inside the network
    And the starter texts are seeded on first start, as locally

  Scenario: Staging is small
    Then one copy each of the frontend and backend run on Fargate, with no NAT gateway and a single-zone database
    And the expected bill is written down in docs/production.md

  # --- Infrastructure as code ---

  Scenario: The whole environment comes from infra/
    When I run "npx cdk synth" in infra/
    Then it produces the staging stack without needing AWS credentials
    And "npm test" in infra/ checks the stack's important properties (as below) without AWS access

  Scenario Outline: The stack's properties are tested
    Then the synthesized stack has <property>

    Examples:
      | property                                                                         |
      | a PostgreSQL database that isn't publicly accessible, with encrypted storage      |
      | database credentials and a login-token secret generated in Secrets Manager        |
      | a backend service reachable only from the frontend's security group               |
      | a load balancer that accepts traffic only from CloudFront                         |
      | a CloudFront distribution redirecting HTTP to HTTPS, with caching off for the app |
      | the prod profile and AUTO_LOGIN=false on the services                             |
      | a GitHub deploy role that only this repository's main branch can assume           |

  # --- Deploying from GitHub ---

  # Since October 2026 a merge is deployed from the developer's PC instead (local-deploy.feature, same steps and site
  # checks); GitHub's deploy below runs only when asked: a hand-started deploy_only run, or DEPLOY_ON_GITHUB=true.

  Scenario: Merging a green pull request deploys staging (when GitHub deploys)
    Given a pull request whose checks all passed
    And the repository variable DEPLOY_ON_GITHUB is "true"
    When it is merged to main
    Then a deploy job signs in to AWS with short-lived credentials (GitHub's OIDC sign-in, no stored keys)
    And builds the images and runs "cdk deploy" for staging
    And checks the site answers afterwards, failing the run if it doesn't
    And checks the backend answers through the site (the public languages list, through CloudFront, the frontend,
      the backend and its database), failing the run if it doesn't

  Scenario: Only main deploys
    When CI runs for a pull request or another branch
    Then no deploy happens
    And a pull request with a red check is never merged (a rule we follow: enforcing it needs GitHub Pro or a public
      repository)

  Scenario: GitHub can deploy by hand, without the checks
    Given I start the CI workflow by hand on main with "deploy_only" ticked
    Then no check runs and staging is deployed from main
    And this is the fallback when the PC can't deploy (local-deploy.feature)
    And on any other branch it deploys nothing

  Scenario: Deploys don't overlap
    When two pushes to main pass CI close together
    Then their deploys run one after the other, never at the same time

  Scenario: Only main can deploy
    When a workflow on any other branch, or a fork, tries to use the deploy role
    Then AWS refuses it

  # --- Known limits of this first staging ---
  # - Uploaded files (audio, profile photos) are on the container's disk and are lost on redeploy: S3 is phase 2.
  # - AI features are off (no OpenAI key on staging yet).
  # - Login is the app's own email-and-password accounts; Cognito replaces it in phase 3.
  # - Dictionary caching is in memory (no Redis) on staging.
